8|0@MMA^1439934139#cu7jc4z:homer_to_the_max$:There's actually an analysis of the knockout somewhere, sorry can't find the link. But right before the left hook, Condit throws an oblique kick to Hardy's lead knee (left leg). That makes Hardy step back his left foot a little bit, forcing him to have an improper stance for a left hook. As both of them are throwing the left hook, Condit, having a wider stance, is able to produce more torque, therefore producing more power. Hence, him being the victor of the exchange, even though they hit each other at the same time. Not only is this a testament to Condit's chin, given Hardy was known for his power, but also his technique.
1|0@MMA^1439989730#cu874g3:cecilsaucer$:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xLyRNMdVehQ This is Coach Winklejohn from Jackson Wink MMA with Los going over the lead hand hook technique showing why Los's left hook on Hardy was so deadly. I love watching Coach Wink break it down. Put a trailer behind your truck!
3|0@MMA^1439940652#cu7n6tn:khabibnurmy$:http://www.bloodyelbow.com/2012/9/1/3282682/judo-chop-jon-jones-signature-techniques-the-jacksons-mma-teep-chasse &gt;The chasse / oblique kick is an extremely useful technique to strikers because it limits the action. Men like Jon Jones and Carlos Condit do well at range when they are dictating what is happening and are landing their biting kicks - they give away all of their advantages on the feet when their opponents are allowed to run at them. &gt; An excellent example of this kick in action is Carlos Condit's use of this kick to defeat Dan Hardy. Watching the fight it was clear that Hardy was the better boxer - hitting Carlos with left hooks every time the latter ran in. Condit's boxing is not great, he throws looping punches with flared elbows and lifts his chin up as he does so - but his kicking game is excellent. Hardy's money punch is his left hook at he gave Condit trouble with it all night. To throw a powerful left hook the weight must start over the left leg, so that one can drive off of it and rotate the left hip and shoulder to the right. By using the oblique kick into Dan Hardy's lead knee repeatedly, Carlos Condit forced Hardy onto his back foot. [Stills of a few Condit chasses](http://assets.sbnation.com/assets/1330116/Condit_1.jpg?_ga=1.89556507.1688300793.1439940400) &gt;In the above stills you will notice that Condit really troubled Hardy as soon as he started throwing his chasse / oblique kick. In the top two stills Hardy attempts a right hand lead, doubtless to set up his left hook, but his knee is almost hyper extended by Condit as Hardy puts his weight forward. In the bottom two stills you can see that Hardy's stance is much shorter and his legs are underneath him, rather than spread to drive off of and create power. [Gif of the finish from a less dumb angle](http://urdirt.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/10/25f5zmc1.gif) &gt; You will notice that Hardy tried to use his usual left hook counter (bottom right still, end of the sequence) but his feet were so close together underneath him that he had no power in his punch, where Condit was driving from his legs and hips with his own left hook. The UFC commentary played up the "Condit got there first" angle, but Hardy's punch connected anyway and had none of it's usual power, simply due to the fact that he was forced out of his usual sprinter's style stance.
1|0@MMA^1439950389#cu7suln:homer_to_the_max$:Yeah, there you go. That's where I read it from.
1|0@MMA^1439944136#cu7p725:just_tweed$:This is the correct response. 
2|0@MMA^1439948785#cu7rwrv:mma_boxing_wrestling$:It's a partial answer. It ignores the fact that Condit's hook really wasn't thrown much better than Hardy's, if at all. He's popping straight up and dragging his right foot. If anything he's putting himself in a very bad position to get hit. Hardy has good rotation and decent weight transfer, so there was absolutely power on his hook, he just doesn't sit down at all. Most importantly, it ignores how the punches actually land. As I said in my other reply, Condit landing cleaner by changing the distance and angle results in Hardy going down while Condit only gets knocked off balance by the forearm. It's not who threw with better mechanics, it's who was more accurate.
2|0@MMA^1439949612#cu7se63:just_tweed$:Is little Slack really disputing big Slack? ;)
7|0@MMA^1439946879#cu7qsei:mma_boxing_wrestling$:Getting Hardy to narrow his stance definitely helped, but the real reason Hardy went down is distance. Hardy had been throwing long hooks all night, and Condit had mostly been pulling back from them. This time, Condit steps forward and slightly off to Hardy's right. Notice that Condit times Hardy's step of his lead foot back into stance with his own forward step, allowing him to get closer than Hardy realizes. Hardy throws another long hook, Condit hooks his short. The difference isn't really how hard either guy threw or how balanced either guy was. The difference is how clean they land, Hardy's fist hits Condit's shoulder/chest, while his forearm makes contact with Condit's face. Condit, on the other hand, lands his knuckles flush on Hardy's jaw. It's two guys getting hit while in bad positions (see how easily Condit is knocked off balance even though he isn't hurt) and one guy landing cleaner than the other, because one guy expected the other to be farther away. 
